<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><channel><title>Build Systems on cesun.life</title><link>https://example.org/tags/build-systems/</link><description>Recent content in Build Systems on cesun.life</description><generator>Hugo</generator><language>en</language><lastBuildDate>Tue, 14 May 2024 21:47:22 +0800</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://example.org/tags/build-systems/index.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><title>GNU Autotools</title><link>https://example.org/gnu-autotools/</link><pubDate>Tue, 14 May 2024 21:47:22 +0800</pubDate><guid>https://example.org/gnu-autotools/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;GNU Autotools generates portable build interfaces from project metadata. Autoconf probes the build environment and emits &lt;code&gt;configure&lt;/code&gt;; Automake generates portable &lt;code&gt;Makefile.in&lt;/code&gt; templates from &lt;code&gt;Makefile.am&lt;/code&gt;; Libtool abstracts platform-specific library linking. The generated files are designed for source releases, whereas maintainers edit the &lt;code&gt;.ac&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;.am&lt;/code&gt;, and m4 inputs.&lt;/p&gt;
